@flexiarg cascades/repointable-declarations
@title Repointable Declarations
@sigils [🧭/改]
@keywords cascade, repoint, revision, tombstone, staleness, filesystem, source-of-truth, edit
@audience knowledge-base designers, agent-system builders
@tone design
@factor Discipline (sīla)
@provenance M-apm-demonstration W.70; Moran 1971 ("transparent beats correct"); the projection-gap lesson (W.43)

! conclusion:
  Repointing a declared edge is a first-class, receipted operation: edit the pattern file's
  upstreams (a reviewed editorial act), let the watcher sync, and have the store repoint —
  retiring the old edge with a tombstone that references the edit, never silently dropping it.

  + context: Declared upstreams go stale — patterns split, merge, or the declared relation
    turns out wrong — and a declaration that cannot be revised will be routed around.

  + IF:
      The filesystem is the source of truth for pattern content and the store mirrors it.

  + HOWEVER:
      If repointing turns out to be FREQUENT (upstreams churning per cycle), the
      file-as-source-of-truth assumption itself is wrong for edges, and edges belong in the
      store natively with files as documentation — watch the repoint rate as a falsifier.

  + THEN:
      Treat upstream edits exactly like pattern authoring: reviewed, committed, ingested;
      the store's sync replaces rather than duplicates, and the retired edge leaves a
      tombstone naming the commit that retired it.

  + BECAUSE:
      Knowledge quality is a property of the revision process, not of any frozen snapshot
      (Moran's best-aged sentence). Silently missing edges poison every measurement downstream
      — the projection gap taught that a graph layer that drops edges unannounced caps what any
      other machinery can see; a graph layer that RETIRES edges announced stays measurable.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Make watcher sync repoint-aware: replace, tombstone, never duplicate or silently drop.]
    next[Track the repoint rate; frequent churn falsifies file-as-source-of-truth for edges.]
